      "text": "\\begin{proof}\nIf an interval $I\\subseteq[1,Z]$ has length $Y$, choosing a largest\ncoordinate of an ordered $j$-tuple whose product belongs to $I$ gives\n\\begin{align*}\n \\sum_{n\\in I}d_j(n)\n &\\le j\\sum_{d\\le Z^{1-1/j}}d_{j-1}(d)(Y/d+1)\\\\\n &\\le j\\{Y(1+\\log Z)^{j-1}\n              +Z^{1-1/j}(1+\\log Z)^{j-2}\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nIndeed the product of the other $j-1$ coordinates is at most\n$Z^{1-1/j}$; dropping the largest-coordinate restriction only increases\nthe sum.  The two final bounds follow by expanding the divisor function\nas an ordered product and summing the last factor.\nFor every integer $e\\ge0$,\n$\\tau(n)^e\\le d_{2^e}(n)$.  On a prime power this follows by mapping",
